export const BRAND_BRIEF = "# A\u00f1ejo Catering Co. \u2014 Brand & Standards Brief\n\n**Source of truth for Creative Studio AI, menu development, kitchen execution, packaging, and customer-facing brand standards.**\n\n## 1. Who we are\n\n**A\u00f1ejo Catering Co. LLC** is one premium Cuban food family in **Palm Beach County, Florida**: **A\u00f1ejo Catering, A\u00f1ejo Traditional, and A\u00f1ejo Fit**. We serve Mediterranean-Cuban bowls, traditional Cuban meals and small bites, individually packed cajitas, and catering for gatherings and teams.\n\n**Owner direction, 2026-09-08:** all three categories deserve prominent food photography and direct shopping paths. La Cajita is our newest family addition, not a fourth competing brand. A\u00f1ejo Bites is not the replacement name for A\u00f1ejo Traditional. MAR (salmon) is the Fit cover; the plated lech\u00f3n with authentic A\u00f1ejo congr\u00ed is the Traditional cover.\n\nCurrent prices, available options, portions, and product images come from active **D1 menu_items / GET /api/menu**. This brief defines identity and standards; historical drafts and campaign documents do not override the live catalog. Read the matching SKU before quoting a price. Launch cost estimates are not verified profit or production yield.\n\n## 2. Vision & mission\n\nBuild a lasting South Florida food brand with Cuban soul, premium presentation, and care in every order: a balanced bowl for a busy day, comforting traditional food, a thoughtful cajita, or a full gathering. The same family serves different occasions without making them compete.\n\nFlavor, hospitality, consistency, and honest product representation come first. Fit supports customers' nutrition goals; Traditional and Catering celebrate Cuban comfort food. Do not apply bowl macro ratios, no-added-sugar claims, or wellness promises to fried bites, desserts, or the whole menu.\n\n## 3. Our three product lines\n\n### A. A\u00f1ejo Catering\n\nTrays, dressed and regular croquetas, empanadas, Hawaiian bocaditos, full skewers, cold salad, cajitas, desserts, and complete spreads. Use tray images and explicitly stated quantities. Regular and dressed croquetas have different prices; the dressed version adds olive, cheese, and spinach. Custom event requests require a reviewed quote.\n\n### B. A\u00f1ejo Traditional\n\nLunch and dinner plates, Cuban sandwiches and tacos, appetizers, individual sides, and desserts. Croquetas and empanadas are one family each with flavor and quantity choices. Tostones rellenos offer ropa vieja, ham and cheese, shrimp, and lech\u00f3n fillings. No standalone salami bite: the full grape\u2013ham/salami\u2013cheese\u2013guava\u2013pineapple skewer appears individually, in La Cajita, or in catering quantities.\n\nMeals include lech\u00f3n, ropa vieja, garbanzos fritos, arroz frito, three chicken or lech\u00f3n tacos, bistec uruguayo with congr\u00ed or rice and beans plus salad, pan con lech\u00f3n, chicken sandwich, chuleta encebollada, potato-and-tuna lasagna, and enchilado de pollo. Published launch portions remain recipe-development assumptions unless specifically owner-confirmed below.\n\n### C. A\u00f1ejo Fit\n\nSeven bowls: VIDA, FUEGO, LIGERO, MAR, COCO, CONGREEN, and RA\u00cdZ; plus Gold Vitality, Hibiscus Zen, and Emerald Hydrate drinks. Standard bowls are 16 oz. Nutrition is approximate. The Macro Portal and goal-sized 12-bowl plans are services within Fit, not a separate family category. Breakfast, shots, and hidden recipe experiments must not be advertised as available without an active catalog entry.\n\n### Shopping and payment routes\n\nThe order menu separates **Lunch & dinner, Appetizers, Sides, Desserts, Catering, A\u00f1ejo Fit, and Sauces**. Flavors and quantities belong inside a family customizer. Optional cart suggestions can pair a bowl with a croqueta, empanada, drink, or dessert; never automatically add or charge an extra.\n\nPriced items go through /order and Square checkout. Traditional and Catering use scheduled delivery; do not promise same-day service. /cajita-builder and /catering collect custom requests for owner review, then a Square deposit and remaining-balance payment link. The quote controls amounts. Card and eligible Apple Pay, Google Pay, and Cash App Pay are supported for one-time checkout; device and eligibility affect visibility. Subscriptions use the recurring flow and eligible card/Apple Pay/Google Pay support; do not promise Cash App or Afterpay for recurring payments. No real settlement test or conversion increase is claimed by this reference update.\n\n## 4. The Golden Rule\n\nFor A\u00f1ejo Fit only, every standard 16 oz bowl targets:\n\n**40% protein / 30% carbs / 30% fat by calories**\n\nWhen scaling, substituting, or creating new bowls, preserve this ratio unless a Macro Portal client's plan specifies otherwise.\n\nNutrition must always be presented as **approximate ranges**, never as precise medical claims. A\u00f1ejo does not diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent disease.\n\n## 5. Culinary house style\n\nThe following nutrition-led culinary system applies to **Fit bowls**. Traditional and Catering follow their own recipes, including cheese, cured meats, fried foods, and desserts; never describe the entire catalog as sugar-free or macro-balanced. Our shared identity is Cuban saz\u00f3n and premium care. Fit's flavor identity is:\n\n* Bright citrus\n* Cuban saz\u00f3n\n* Mediterranean freshness\n* Chimichurri and house A\u00f1ejo sauces\n* Quinoa-forward and clean grain bases\n* High-protein builds\n* Anti-inflammatory ingredients where possible\n* Generous fiber\n* Fresh herbs, acids, spices, seeds, and microgreens\n* Sauce served on the side unless a photo or plated sample requires controlled drizzle\n* No added sugars or unhealthy oils and fats\n* Smoke-infused extra virgin olive oil\n\nThe food should taste craveable first, then reveal itself as healthy.\n\n### Texture and temperature standards\n\n* Proteins must be seared, grilled, roasted, or cooked with visible culinary intention \u2014 never pale, watery, or steamed-looking.\n* Chicken should be sliced cleanly on the bias and look juicy.\n* Steak should have color, char, and structure.\n* Shrimp should look glossy, firm, and premium.\n* Salmon and tuna should be the hero visually and nutritionally.\n* Greens must stay crisp and never soggy.\n* Vegetables should be bright, clean-cut, and sectioned.\n* Bases should be fluffy, not wet or compressed.\n* Seeds, nuts, microgreens, and toppings are used to create texture and premium finish.\n\n### Signature techniques and ingredients\n\nA\u00f1ejo is known for:\n\n* Clockwise sectional bowl plating\n* Hero protein placement from approximately 5 o'clock to 7 o'clock\n* Controlled sauce identity\n* Microgreens as a premium finishing signature or main salad\n* Citrus-forward sauces\n* Cuban-inspired seasoning layered into clean ingredients\n* Mediterranean structure with Cuban flavor memory\n* Premium abundance without messy overcrowding\n\n## 6. Menu\n\n### Serving and pricing authority \u2014 owner update, 2026-09-08\n\nOwner-confirmed: ensalada fr\u00eda 6 oz; croqueta 1.20 oz after frying; empanada 1.25 oz; lech\u00f3n 6 oz per serving; tamal three slices approximately 5.5 oz; congr\u00ed 8 oz **by weight**. Do not equate the rice weight with two cups. The 6 oz salad instruction means cold salad, not fresh salad. Other proposed portions require production validation.\n\nCold salad is $5 individually; the 10-serving tray is $45. Regular croqueta trays are $40/25 and $75/50; dressed trays are $65/25 and $125/50. These are dated reference examples; re-read live SKU prices before publishing. Strawberry and chocolate tres leches each have individual cups and whole round cakes; catering cup packs are separate quantities. Sides use per-person portions and images, not catering trays.\n\nThe full catalog and serving-specific asset reference is in Hub's **Menu, Photography & Marketing Reference \u2014 September 2026**. Its price table is a dated snapshot; /api/menu remains current authority. Production costs, food-cost percentages, nutrition and margin estimates are not audited results.\n\n### Current public bowls\n\n**COCO \u2014 Coconut Lime Shrimp Bowl**\nQuinoa, corn, edamame, coconut-lime shrimp, avocado, cucumber, tomato, spinach, pickled onion, sesame, toasted coconut, microgreens, and coconut-lime sauce.\n\n**LIGERO \u2014 Grilled Chicken Wellness Bowl**\nQuinoa, golden grilled chicken, greens, tomato, cucumber, roasted vegetables or sweet potato, apple-almond accent, toasted almonds, microgreens, and Golden Turmeric dressing.\n\n**CONGREEN \u2014 Tuna Green Bowl**\nQuinoa base, tuna, avocado, cucumber, cherry tomatoes, greens, black beans, pickled onion, seed mix, microgreens, and Ajo C\u00edtrico dressing.\n\n**FUEGO \u2014 Steak Bowl**\nQuinoa, seared steak, arugula, roasted peppers, roasted zucchini, pickled onion, pepitas, microgreens, and Chimichurri Vital.\n\n**VIDA \u2014 Fresh Tuna Mango Bowl**\nQuinoa, fresh tuna, mango, chickpeas, greens, cherry tomatoes, cucumber, seed mix, microgreens, and Mango Omega sauce.\n\n**MAR \u2014 Salmon Bowl**\nPremium salmon bowl built around A\u00f1ejo's clean omega-forward identity, with a balanced grain base, fresh vegetables, bright sauce, seeds, and microgreens.\n\n**RA\u00cdZ \u2014 Crispy Tofu Bowl**\nPlant-forward bowl with crispy tofu as the hero protein, quinoa or clean grain base, fresh vegetables, fiber-rich toppings, controlled sauce, and microgreens.\n\n### Spec / hidden / not-yet-launched bowls\n\n**FUERZA \u2014 Pork Loin Performance Bowl**\nGrilled pork loin, rice or quinoa, spinach-tomato salad, dried onion, queso fresco, almonds, pumpkin seeds, and a clean creamy dressing profile. FUERZA may remain in internal specs until ready for public launch.\n\n### Seasonal / rotating items\n\nSeasonal rotations may include:\n\n* New protein bowls\n* Limited sauces\n* Seasonal tropical fruit accents\n* Gym-partner exclusive bowls\n* Corporate wellness menus\n* Macro Portal custom variations\n* A\u00f1ejo Fit Drinks: Gold Vitality, Hibiscus Zen, and Emerald Hydrate (use current catalog names)\n\nSeasonal items follow the relevant category's plating, quality, and allergen standards; Fit bowl macro targets apply only to Fit bowls.\n\n## 7. Ingredient & sourcing standards\n\n### Quality bar\n\nA\u00f1ejo ingredients must look, taste, and perform like premium food.\n\n* Fresh produce whenever possible\n* Local sourcing when quality, consistency, and pricing allow\n* Organic where it matters most and is operationally realistic\n* Proteins must be high quality and handled carefully\n* No cheap-looking fillers\n* No watery vegetables\n* No wilted greens\n* No bland \"meal prep\" execution\n* Every ingredient must earn its place visually, nutritionally, and culinarily\n\n### Preferred ingredients\n\n* Quinoa and clean grain bases\n* Lean proteins\n* Salmon, tuna, shrimp, chicken, steak, tofu, pork loin\n* Greens, cucumber, tomatoes, avocado, mango, beans, chickpeas, roasted vegetables\n* Citrus, herbs, garlic, olive-oil-based sauces, chimichurri-style sauces\n* Seeds, almonds, pepitas, sesame, microgreens\n* Anti-inflammatory spices such as turmeric where appropriate\n\n### Ingredients to avoid or restrict\n\n* Excess added sugar\n* Heavy seed-oil-forward cooking\n* Greasy sauces\n* Overly processed ingredients\n* Artificial-tasting dressings\n* Cheap fillers used only to increase volume\n* Ingredients that make the bowl look wet, sloppy, or low-end\n\nPortioning for each standard bowl is **16 oz**. Exact ingredient weights come from the kitchen manual and production tickets.\n\n## 8. Allergens & dietary rules\n\nAllergen discipline is non-negotiable.\n\n### Hard allergen rules\n\n* Allergens must be clearly identified on recipes, labels, menus, and production sheets.\n* Always label or disclose: nuts, tree nuts, sesame, egg, dairy, gluten/wheat, fish, shellfish, and soy when present or possible through supplier cross-contact.\n* Never assume a customer \"can probably have it.\"\n* Never suggest substitutions that conflict with a recorded allergy or preference.\n* If a client has recorded allergens, those override flavor, presentation, and general menu standards.\n* Sauce and toppings must be checked separately, not just the main bowl.\n* Nuts and seeds must be removable where possible, but cross-contact must still be disclosed if the kitchen is not fully allergen-free.\n\n### Current A\u00f1ejo-specific allergen notes\n\n* Croquetas are not b\u00e9chamel-based.\n* Croqueta masa is all-purpose flour-based.\n* Croquetas do not contain milk/dairy in the masa unless the specific item contains cheese.\n* Breaded items may use egg wash.\n* No soy is intentionally added unless a specific supplier ingredient contains it.\n* Only items containing cheese or dairy-based sauces should be labeled as dairy-containing.\n* Supplier cross-contact risk must still be acknowledged.\n\n### Cross-contamination standards\n\n* Use clean gloves, utensils, boards, and containers for allergen-sensitive orders.\n* Separate nut/seed toppings from nut-free orders.\n* Keep sauces labeled and closed.\n* Package allergen-sensitive orders separately.\n* Mark modified orders clearly on production tickets and labels.\n* When in doubt, disclose instead of guessing.\n\n## 9. Food-safety & cold-chain standards\n\nCold-chain and food safety are operational law.\n\n* Coolers must read **\u2264 41\u00b0F** at loadout and dropoff.\n* Record temperatures at both ends.\n* Hot items must be held at **\u2265 135\u00b0F**.\n* Follow Kitchen Opening, sanitation, batch labeling, cooling, storage, and delivery-safety procedures in the Standards Library.\n* Label batches with production date, item, handler, and shelf-life.\n* Standard shelf life is **3 days** unless the kitchen manual states otherwise.\n* Any item outside safe temperature, quality, or visual standard must not be served.\n\n## 10. Plating & presentation\n\nA correct A\u00f1ejo bowl should look premium before the customer takes a bite.\n\n### Signature bowl layout\n\n* Use clockwise sectional plating.\n* The protein is the hero and usually sits from **5 o'clock to 7 o'clock**.\n* The base should support the bowl without hiding the premium ingredients.\n* Vegetables are arranged in clean, intentional sections.\n* Avocado, mango, tomatoes, cucumber, greens, beans, chickpeas, roasted vegetables, and toppings should each have visual space.\n* Microgreens finish every premium bowl unless unavailable or inappropriate for the specific build.\n* Seeds, nuts, sesame, pepitas, or coconut are used as controlled texture accents.\n* The bowl should look abundant but clean \u2014 never empty, never chaotic.\n\n### Photo standard\n\nMatch the purchased serving format: catering trays for catering, single bites and cream plates for appetizers/sides, cups for individual desserts, round cakes for whole cakes. Cuban pizza must use the plated image without a metal mold, showing crisp cheesy borders. Congr\u00ed must retain the owner's purple-brown color, distinct long rice grains, black beans and golden cracklings; avoid pale or mushy substitutes. Croquetas are elongated oval Cuban croquetas. Combo images must show their described components.\n\nUse the approved product's existing catalog image before generating another. Keep the cream, forest-green, charcoal and gold palette; pink packaging is an event theme. Food must remain the hero. Lifestyle imagery must not imply unverified clients or events. AI-enhanced or generated illustrations must not be represented as documentary photos of delivered orders. Existing homepage slideshow uses A\u00f1ejo food/event images; no AI people video is required.\n\n\n* Full round bowl visible for Fit; complete plate, cup, box, or tray visible for other formats.\n* Bowl front and center.\n* Premium matte black, dark forest green, cream, or neutral backgrounds.\n* Lighting should feel luxury, editorial, and fresh.\n* Ingredients must look bright and high-quality.\n* Sauce may be shown as a controlled drizzle for photography, but operationally sauce is usually served on the side.\n* No messy edges.\n* No overfilled lids.\n* No soggy greens.\n* No cheap takeout look.\n\n### Packaging standard\n\nA\u00f1ejo packaging should feel more premium than ordinary delivery food.\n\n* Clean bowl presentation\n* Round 3-inch lid label when used\n* Bowl name clearly visible\n* Client name where possible\n* QR code may link to bowl breakdown, macros, and review request\n* Sauce cup should be labeled or visually paired with the bowl\n* Delivery should feel like receiving a premium wellness product, not just lunch\n\n## 11. Brand voice\n\n### Tone\n\nA\u00f1ejo speaks with warmth, confidence, and expertise.\n\nThe voice is:\n\n* Premium but not arrogant\n* Health-smart but not clinical\n* Flavor-first but disciplined\n* Cuban-rooted but modern\n* Confident but never fake\n* Emotional when telling the founder story\n* Clean and direct when describing food\n\nA\u00f1ejo should never sound like generic meal prep, gym bro food, or hospital food.\n\n### Naming convention\n\nBowls use bold, memorable Spanish names that feel strong, clean, and brandable.\n\nExamples:\n\n* COCO\n* LIGERO\n* CONGREEN\n* FUEGO\n* VIDA\n* MAR\n* RA\u00cdZ\n* FUERZA\n\nNames should be short, iconic, easy to say, and emotionally connected to the bowl's identity.\n\n### Description style\n\nFood descriptions should be sensory and benefit-driven without making medical claims.\n\nUse language like:\n\n* Fresh\n* Balanced\n* Protein-forward\n* Citrus-bright\n* Clean\n* Craveable\n* Goal-aligned\n* Omega-rich\n* Fiber-rich\n* Chef-built\n* Cuban-inspired\n* Mediterranean-rooted\n* Premium wellness bowl\n\n### Words and claims we avoid\n\nAvoid:\n\n* \"Diet food\"\n* \"Weight loss guaranteed\"\n* \"Cures\"\n* \"Treats\"\n* \"Prevents disease\"\n* \"Medical meal\"\n* \"Doctor-approved\" unless actually documented\n* \"Exact macros\" when using estimates\n* \"No risk\" allergen language\n* Cheap hype such as \"best ever\" without context\n* Overpromising transformation results\n\n### The feeling underneath the voice \u2014 Reposado (Dayan, 2026-07-27)\n\nA\u00f1ejo means aged. **Reposado means well rested** \u2014 staying still, relaxed, just existing, chilling,\ntaking a deep pause. That is the feeling every piece of A\u00f1ejo work should carry underneath the\nwords, whether or not anyone ever names it.\n\n**A\u00f1ejo governs brand-wide. Reposado is an input, not the ruling document.** Where the two ever\ndisagree, A\u00f1ejo wins \u2014 \"Reposado is good, but A\u00f1ejo is better.\" This settles the scope question:\nthe visual-philosophy doc informs the work; it does not govern it.\n\n**Light: both, coexisting.** Not dark backgrounds with bright food, and not the bright-everything\nof the older photography standard. *\"The perfect mix of bright and dark, they both coexist, make it\nwork.\"* The vault Photography Standards (\"no harsh shadows\") and the Reposado language (\"quiet and\ndark like a cellar at dusk\") had been read as a conflict to resolve one way or the other \u2014 Dayan\nrefused the either/or, and both are now in force together.\n\n**The words stay internal.** *\"Cultivated stillness is a pretty cool philosophy. For now the\nillustrations are fine \u2014 I have to live it more before I feel like sharing the words.\"*\n\nSo: the philosophy shapes tone, pacing, imagery and restraint **silently**. The phrase \"cultivate\nstillness\" (or \"cultivated stillness\") must **not** appear in menus, captions, the site, decks or\nany customer-facing copy until Dayan says otherwise. Illustrations carrying the feeling are\napproved for use now. Full working system: `_AI_COLLABORATION_COMMAND_CENTER/marketing/ANEJO_CULTIVATE_STILLNESS.md`.\nRuling of record: `01 Source of Truth/RULINGS.md` R-0031.\n\n## 12. Non-negotiables\n\nThe AI, kitchen, and brand must always honor the following:\n\n1. Standard \u00e0 la carte bowls are **16 oz**.\n2. Standard bowls follow the Golden Rule: **40% protein / 30% carbs / 30% fat** unless a Macro Portal plan overrides it.\n3. Macro Portal client targets override general culinary defaults.\n4. Allergens override everything.\n5. Nutrition is always approximate and never presented as a medical claim.\n6. Sauce is served on the side unless a specific photo, event, or plated sample requires controlled drizzle.\n7. Every bowl must look premium, abundant, clean, and intentional.\n8. Microgreens are part of the A\u00f1ejo premium visual signature whenever available.\n9. No bowl should look like basic meal prep.\n10. Cuban flavor and Mediterranean nutrition must work together \u2014 never one without the other.\n11. Packaging must reinforce the premium experience.\n12. The brand must protect trust: honest ingredients, clean execution, safe handling, clear labeling, and no exaggerated claims.\n13. A\u00f1ejo is built for legacy \u2014 every recipe, photo, label, sauce, bowl, delivery, and customer interaction should feel like it belongs to a brand that can scale.\n";
